  {
   "id": "hxEJF9DJ3fwJ",
   "from": "Brandon Hurr",
   "author": "27405c4b711a1080",
   "date": "2010-12-27T15:45:47Z",
   "subject": "Re: how to plot on left and right side y -axis",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>data&lt;-structure(list(year = c(2007L, 2008L, 2009L, 2007L, 2008L, 2009L, </div><div>2007L, 2008L, 2009L), variable = structure(c(1L, 1L, 1L, 2L, </div><div>2L, 2L, 3L, 3L, 3L), .Label = c(&quot;x1&quot;, &quot;x2&quot;, &quot;y1&quot;), class = &quot;factor&quot;), </div>\n\n<div>    value = c(10L, 11L, 13L, 18L, 19L, 21L, 123L, 124L, 130L), </div><div>    facet.var = c(1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 1L, 2L, 2L, 2L)), .Names = c(&quot;year&quot;, </div><div>&quot;variable&quot;, &quot;value&quot;, &quot;facet.var&quot;), class = &quot;data.frame&quot;, row.names = c(NA, </div>\n\n<div>-9L))</div><div><div><br></div><div>ggplot(data=data, aes(year, value, colour=variable))+geom_line()</div></div><div><br></div><div>I can see why you want to use another scale for your data. The y data for the one variable is 1 log10 higher than the other. </div>\n\n<div><br></div>I&#39;m pretty sure there is still no way within ggplot to have more than one axis scale, and I believe that this is because it is quite difficult, but perhaps more importantly, it is very misleading. One way around this could be to plot the x&#39;s and the y&#39;s as two plots independently and then put them next to each other. I&#39;d love to be wrong here since there have been times where two scales would have been useful. <div>\n\n<br></div><div>-B<br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Mon, Dec 27, 2010 at 10:38, SNV Krishna <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">kri...@primps.com.sg</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
